WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.871
An academic receives an email from a second-language

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:07.719
writer and answers not its argument but an accusation

00:00:07.719 --> 00:00:09.429
about the writing tool.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.786
A stranger receives a professional message. Instead of

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:23.121
answering what it says, he becomes interested in

00:00:23.121 --> 00:00:25.206
whether software touched its grammar.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.873
Someone may write in a second language, seek clarity

00:00:34.873 --> 00:00:38.297
and still be treated as if the improvement itself

00:00:38.297 --> 00:00:39.438
discredits the speaker.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.151
Before mocking a junior colleague, imagine announcing

00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:52.623
to your boss or an established native speaker that

00:00:52.623 --> 00:00:55.324
their polished prose makes them a bot.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.991
For years academics were instructed, and sometimes

00:01:05.991 --> 00:01:09.533
charged, to have manuscripts professionally edited. The

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.074
expectation of standardized language did not originate

00:01:13.074 --> 00:01:14.086
with AI.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.200
The same professional register appears in messages from

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.481
native speakers and people with greater authority.

00:01:25.481 --> 00:01:27.355
Notice who gets challenged.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.220
Software assistance does not automatically certify a

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.528
bad argument; nor does an unedited mistake certify

00:01:40.528 --> 00:01:41.067
originality.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.982
Criticise ideas when they deserve criticism. A polished

00:01:49.982 --> 00:01:54.397
sentence is not proof that its writer has no mind.
